BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:tech-policy-press-fellowship-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260827T203244Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260924
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260925
SUMMARY:Deadline: Tech Policy Press Fellowship Program 2027
DESCRIPTION:Tech Policy Press is recruiting its 2027 fellowship cohort to p
 ursue independent reporting and analysis on urgent questions at the inters
 ection of technology and democracy. Since 2023 the programme has supported
  25 fellows from 11 countries across three cohorts\, writing on AI governa
 nce\, tech regulation\, digital rights and the entanglement of state and c
 orporate power. WHO IT IS FOR: mid-career journalists\, researchers\, lawy
 ers and public policy professionals with a strong background in a specific
  tech policy area\, a particular geography\, or technology's impact on dem
 ocracy and society. Applicants from the Global Majority and from communiti
 es underrepresented in media are encouraged\, as are projects that centre 
 communities and regions usually excluded from global tech policy conversat
 ions. Contributions can range from original reporting to policy analysis\,
  and technical practitioners are welcome as long as the work fits the publ
 ication's editorial purview. EXPECTATIONS: at least one piece of analysis 
 or reporting per month (exclusive to Tech Policy Press\, though it may be 
 republished elsewhere)\, a mandatory hour-long monthly cohort meeting\, a 
 monthly check-in with the editorial team\, collaboration with other fellow
 s and the masthead\, agreed deadlines\, and disclosure of conflicts of int
 erest. Applicants should arrive with a clear area of focus and a sense of 
 how the work will inform both the public and decision-makers. APPLY via th
 e online form by 11:59pm EDT on 24 September 2026\, with a CV or resume\, 
 a cover letter of no more than two pages\, links to prior publications (op
 en access\, published within the past three years\, at least one in Englis
 h and at least one solo-authored\; social posts\, pre-prints and unpublish
 ed coursework do not count) and two professional references. Shortlisted c
 andidates may be invited to an online interview. Decisions are expected by
  early December 2026. Two optional Zoom information sessions run on 9 Sept
 ember at 8am EDT and 16 September at 7pm EDT\; registration required.\n\nO
 rganisation: Tech Policy Press\nLocation: Fully remote\, worldwide. Intern
 ational applicants explicitly encouraged\; fellows work as independent con
 tractors. No requirement to be authorised to work in the USA.\nAward: USD 
 10\,000 stipend per fellow\, paid quarterly. Also included: editorial guid
 ance from the Tech Policy Press team\, membership of an international coho
 rt with regular feedback and exchange\, and monthly sessions to strengthen
  the work. Fellows' work is published on the Tech Policy Press website and
  promoted through the newsletter\, podcast and events. No application fee.
 \nDuration: Twelve months\, part-time\, running January to December 2027\n
 \nMore: https://www.techpolicy.press/call-for-applications-2027-tech-polic
 y-press-fellowship-program/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medi
 um=referral
URL:https://www.techpolicy.press/call-for-applications-2027-tech-policy-pre
 ss-fellowship-program/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=re
 ferral
LOCATION:Fully remote\, worldwide. International applicants explicitly enco
 uraged\; fellows work as independent contractors. No requirement to be aut
 horised to work in the USA.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:iac-bc-ai-builders-fellowship-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260827T203244Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260821
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260822
SUMMARY:Deadline: AI Builders Fellowship 2026 (Internet Archive Canada x BC
  + AI)
DESCRIPTION:A $5\,000 fellowship to spend six weeks turning a very large Ca
 nadian public archive into something new. THE MATERIAL: Internet Archive C
 anada's Government Publications collection\, part of the Democracy's Libra
 ry project\, holding over 104\,000 text items\, roughly 22.1 million scann
 ed pages and about 48.25 TB of Canadian public-record material\, browsable
  at archive.org/details/governmentpublications. WHAT TO BUILD: deliberatel
 y open-ended. The call names a search tool that answers a real question wi
 th page-level citations\, a timeline tracing one policy across decades of 
 government language\, and a sonic or visual piece built from archival mate
 rial\, but explicitly invites work nobody has thought of yet. The requirem
 ents are that it is useful\, that it is imaginative\, and that the code is
  open source when it is done. ORIGIN: the fellowship came out of conversat
 ions between BC + AI's Kris Kruger and IAC executive director Andrea Mills
  about treating a public archive as material for new work rather than only
  something to preserve. IAC brings the archive\, BC + AI brings a communit
 y of builders used to working in public. ELIGIBILITY: anyone\, anywhere in
  the world\; one builder is selected. APPLICATION: a specific project desc
 ription grounded in the collection\, a phase-by-phase work plan for the si
 x weeks\, and a success metric\, submitted at bc-ai.ca/fellowship/apply. K
 EY DATES: applications close 21 August 2026 at 11:59pm Pacific\; fellow se
 lected by 31 August\; build runs 1 September to mid-October\; showcase at 
 Futureproof Festival in Vancouver on 28 October 2026 if the project is rea
 dy.\n\nOrganisation: Internet Archive Canada (IAC) in partnership with BC 
 + AI\nLocation: Remote\, open to builders anywhere in the world. Optional 
 in-person showcase at Futureproof Festival in Vancouver\, Canada on 28 Oct
 ober 2026 if the project is ready.\nAward: $5\,000 award for one selected 
 builder. No application fee.\nDuration: Six-week build\, 1 September to mi
 d-October 2026\, remote\, with support from BC + AI throughout\n\nMore: ht
 tps://bc-ai.ca/fellowship?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium
 =referral
URL:https://bc-ai.ca/fellowship?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_m
 edium=referral
LOCATION:Remote\, open to builders anywhere in the world. Optional in-perso
 n showcase at Futureproof Festival in Vancouver\, Canada on 28 October 202
 6 if the project is ready.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:surface-design-association-air-2026-27@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260827T203244Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260814
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260815
SUMMARY:Deadline: Surface Design Association: Artist in Residence 2026-27 (
 virtual)
DESCRIPTION:The Surface Design Association's Artist in Residence programme 
 is built to develop skills\, open up relationships and support fiber and t
 extile artists through a studio project that pushes their practice. The re
 sidency is entirely virtual and runs across a full year\, which is the poi
 nt: it removes the travel\, housing and time-away barriers that keep many 
 practitioners out of in-person residencies. One artist is selected annuall
 y by a panel of SDA staff\, board members and committee volunteers. WHAT I
 T INVOLVES: studio visits across the year\, social media programming\, and
  meetings with the SDA community\, plus further programming shaped around 
 the resident's own skills and interests\, which can include teaching onlin
 e workshops\, contributing to SDA publications\, jurying exhibitions or jo
 ining panels. FUNDING: a $2\,000 studio stipend for the project\, plus up 
 to $2\,000 in additional compensation for programming work\, targeting abo
 ut $4\,000 total. ELIGIBILITY: emerging\, mid-career and established artis
 ts\; US-based and international applicants welcome\; students and professi
 onals may apply\; applicants must hold current SDA membership (from $85 pe
 r year\, $60 for students). APPLICATION: a portfolio of 10 images with ful
 l artwork information (title\, year\, materials\, technique\, dimensions\,
  photo credit)\, a detailed CV\, essay responses on artistic goals\, progr
 amming interests and professional development\, and demographic informatio
 n. No application fee. The 2026-27 cycle closes 14 August 2026.\n\nOrganis
 ation: Surface Design Association (SDA)\nLocation: Fully virtual. Open to 
 US-based and international applicants\; current SDA membership is required
 .\nAward: $2\,000 studio stipend\, plus up to $2\,000 further compensation
  for programming work (SDA aims for roughly $4\,000 in total per resident)
 . No application fee\, but applicants must be current SDA members (members
 hip from $85 per year\, $60 for students).\nDuration: One year\, virtual\,
  October 2026 to August 2027\n\nMore: https://www.surfacedesign.org/air_fo
 rm/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.surfacedesign.org/air_form/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugra
 ntdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Fully virtual. Open to US-based and international applicants\; cur
 rent SDA membership is required.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:global-artivism-governance-fellow-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260827T203244Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260716
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260717
SUMMARY:Deadline: Global Artivism: Governance Fellow
DESCRIPTION:Global Artivism\, a Global Majority-led movement that engages t
 he power of arts and culture toward a just world on a safe planet\, is con
 tracting a Governance Fellow for a mentored\, time-limited engagement focu
 sed on governance systems\, democratic institutional design\, and Global M
 ajority-led movement infrastructure. The contractor supports the developme
 nt and maintenance of Global Artivism's governance architecture\, includin
 g documentation\, committee coordination\, and research that supports the 
 Democratic Transition process\, and is expected to take initiative on assi
 gned tasks and grow through structured mentorship. The movement is governe
 d by a Global Council with a Movement Secretariat coordination team\; the 
 Fellow coordinates primarily with the Governance Lead alongside the Operat
 ions Director\, Secretariat members\, and Council committees (coordination
  relationships do not constitute supervisory authority). KEY RESPONSIBILIT
 IES: governance documentation (frameworks\, committee charters\, terms of 
 reference\, process documentation\; maintaining an organized\, current\, c
 onsistently formatted document library\; preparing materials for Global Co
 uncil meetings)\; committee coordination (scheduling\, materials\, meeting
  notes\, follow-up\, trackers of decisions and action items\, agendas and 
 briefing packs)\; Democratic Transition and electoral process support (res
 earch on governance models\, electoral processes\, and democratic particip
 ation mechanisms\; drafting materials for the Democratic Transition Roadma
 p 2025-2027\; preparing electoral framework and testing documentation\; tr
 acking milestones)\; and governance research (comparative analyses and bri
 efing notes\; staying current on distributed governance\, participatory de
 mocracy\, and decentralized leadership\; contributing to knowledge product
 s). REQUIRED: manages multiple documentation tasks and meets deadlines wit
 h attention to accuracy and formatting (experience via union organizing\, 
 participatory facilitation\, cooperative governance\, or community-based d
 ecision-making counts alongside academic or formal work history)\; good wr
 itten English\; ability to take initiative and work independently\; genuin
 e interest in governance\, movement building\, or institutional design\; a
 bility to operate as an independent contractor\; and commitment to Global 
 Artivism's values. PREFERRED: background in governance\, law\, political s
 cience\, or international development\; interest in Global Majority leader
 ship and distributed power\; civil society/NGO/movement experience\; famil
 iarity with arts\, culture\, or social justice movements\; multilingual ab
 ility in languages relevant to Global South contexts. This is an independe
 nt contractor engagement managed through Global Artivism's fiscal sponsors
 hip with Res Publica US Inc.\; the contractor sets their own hours and met
 hods\, may serve other clients\, and handles their own taxes and local com
 pliance. TO APPLY: submit an expression of interest via the ABM Intelligen
 ce application page\; submissions are reviewed on a rolling basis by ABM's
  consulting team. Expressions of interest close Thursday 16 July 2026\, 10
 pm MYT (UTC+8)\; late submissions will not be reviewed. Priority is given 
 to candidates from the Global South and Global Majority communities\, and 
 community organizing\, grassroots coordination\, and self-taught practice 
 are valued alongside formal credentials.\n\nOrganisation: Global Artivism 
 (a fiscally sponsored project of Res Publica US Inc.)\nLocation: Remote\, 
 open globally. Priority given to applicants from the Global South.\nAward:
  USD 1\,200 to 1\,800 per month (independent contractor fee)\, paid on rec
 eipt of monthly invoices. No employee benefits\, paid leave\, or insurance
 \; contractor covers their own taxes and compliance.\nDuration: 12-month i
 ndependent contractor engagement from August 2026\, with potential renewal
  subject to funding and mutual agreement. Flexible hours with scheduled ca
 lls across time zones.\n\nMore: https://www.abm-intelligence.com/governanc
 efellow-globalartivism?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=re
 ferral
URL:https://www.abm-intelligence.com/governancefellow-globalartivism?utm_so
 urce=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Remote\, open globally. Priority given to applicants from the Glob
 al South.
END:VEVENT
